NAME
       ln - make links between files

SYNOPSIS
       ln -s [-fvnT] TARGET [LINK_NAME]
       ln -s [-fvn] TARGET... DIRECTORY

DESCRIPTION
       Create a symbolic link LINK_NAME that points to TARGET. With one
       operand the link is created in the current directory under the
       target's base name. When the last operand is a directory, a link
       for each TARGET is created inside it.

       TARGET is stored as written. A relative TARGET is resolved from the
       directory that holds the link, not from the current directory.
       Following a chain of links stops after 40 steps with ELOOP.

       Hard links are not supported yet: ln without -s reports an error.

OPTIONS
       -s, --symbolic
           make a symbolic link

       -f, --force
           remove an existing LINK_NAME first (never a directory)

       -n, --no-dereference
           treat a LINK_NAME that is a link to a directory as a file

       -T
           always treat LINK_NAME as a file name, never as a directory

       -v, --verbose
           print each link that is created

EXAMPLES
       ln -s libpg.so.2.2.3 /usr/lib/libpg.so.2
       ln -sf /usr/bin/vim /usr/local/bin/vi

SEE ALSO
       readlink(1), realpath(1), ls(1), stat(1)
